From a0bdf74c361f012a635a8511f77be35827576cdf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:19:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: draw frames without GUI elements the way that is
 faster on this GPU

A Dolby Vision frame without GUI elements is drawn directly (the shader
writes the transport into the output); with the player bar, subtitles
or any other GUI element it is composed (reconstructed into the
full-precision picture, the GUI blended in, then packed). Both pack the
same reconstruction, each with a shader of its own (drawn by a software
renderer, under 0.1 % of the pixels came out one code of 4096 apart,
and no metadata bit), and the renderer already goes from one to the
other whenever the bar appears.

Direct drawing was made as the fast path, and on a discrete GPU it is:
7 to 9 ms of GPU time against 11 to 18 on an RTX 4080 SUPER. On the
integrated GPU of a Ryzen 9 7950X3D, with a FEL stream, it is the slow
one: 35 to 37 ms (up to 44) against 30, of the 41.7 ms of a refresh at
23.976 Hz. In a session of 2026-10-03 one frame in three missed its
refresh (167 to 179 frames shown every 10 s instead of 240), and
playback became smooth whenever the player bar was on the screen.

The GPU time of each frame was already measured for the statistics.
CDVPathChoice now takes it for the frames without GUI elements.

What a frame costs depends on the size of its picture and of its active
area, and on whether the residual of its enhancement layer is drawn:
its load. The active area counts in 64ths of the picture, and a frame
within one 64th of the frame with which the last load began is of that
load (a few lines more or less from one shot to the next are the same
work, also at 3840x1600 and 3840x2076, which are within four lines of
where the 64ths divide). Each load is measured by itself, eight at a
time: its windows are made of its own frames whenever they come, and
once it has had a comparison, nothing that it measures or decides
depends on which other loads are on the screen, on when they are, or on
what they take. Rules that make one choice for all loads at a time had
the loads hold each other up: a heavier picture shape in shots too
short for a comparison began one at every return, one that returned for
eight frames every forty seconds held the comparison of the film for
fifty minutes, and two shapes of the same work could wait for each
other for good.

The way a load is drawn is measured in windows of 48 frames whose
slowest frame is left out. When it takes more than 60 % of the refresh
period, the other way is used for one window. If that looks better (a
gain under 5 % is not worth a change), the way that was in use is
measured for one more window, and the other one is taken only when it
beats both: one slow window of the way in use, when something else held
the GPU or the processor, decides nothing. From then on the load has a
way of its own. Simulated with times that come back two frames late,
that is 111 frames when the way in use is kept and 167 when the other
one is taken: on the integrated GPU, seven seconds at the first FEL
video of a run of Kodi, under five of them drawn the slow way.

One comparison is not believed for good. A window of either way can be
slow for a reason that has nothing to do with the way, most of all in
the first seconds of a video, which is when the first comparison is
made. The way that won is compared again a minute later, whatever its
time is, and again until it has won twice in a row. Simulated with 6 ms
more on every frame while the other way was tried: direct drawing is
kept at first, and composing taken 70 s into the film (1616 of the
172800 frames of two hours drawn the slow way). The other way round, on
a fast GPU with two bursts of 20 ms placed in 201 ways around a
comparison, 88 of them made it take composing, and in every one direct
drawing was back within 70 s. After that a way known to be slower is
compared again only when the one in use has become slower than the
other one was, or, once in a video, when it no longer fits in the
period at all. Between two comparisons of a load 1440 frames pass
(measured or not: a minute of film), and twice as many after each one
that asked nothing new, up to sixteen times.

A load that has not had a comparison of its own is drawn the shared
way: the one that the last comparison to be confirmed found, for
whichever load (direct drawing before the first one). So a load that is
drawn in a small part of the period is never compared, and on a GPU
that draws every load so, nothing is ever tried: nothing changes there.
A load that takes most of the period is compared, unless it takes no
more (to 5 %) than the heaviest load that has confirmed the shared way
did: what a picture that is as much work or more has found is not tried
again for every picture shape, and a trial is two seconds of what may
be the slower way. That holds under four fifths of the period: a load
that takes more is compared for itself. (On the integrated GPU, of the
240 refreshes of ten seconds, none to two presentations were late at 72
to 75 % of the period, and 61 to 73 at 84 to 88 %. Nothing was measured
between the two, and four fifths is the middle.) A later look that
keeps a way does not change what the shared way was confirmed with: it
is made when the way has become slower, in a burst as likely as not. A
comparison that has not been confirmed is the way of its own load only:
a wrong one is not passed on.

The shared way and the ways that loads have found stay from one video
to the next, for each of the two kinds of stream (with and without an
enhancement layer), which load the GPU differently. What was being
measured does not: when native output ends, the windows are dropped and
a comparison that had not ended is given up. In the first minute of a
video nothing that is known is put in question: its first windows can
be slow for what starting it costs (the decoder fills its buffers on
the same GPU), and a trial of the slow way would be made at every
start. A load of which nothing is known is compared with its first
window all the same. Simulated with composing confirmed and twenty
videos whose first 60 to 300 frames take 20 to 45 % more: no trial,
where there were 66 in nine such runs.

The way of a frame is that of its own load, which its metadata tells
before the frame is drawn (CDVBridgeGLES::Foresee(): the level 5
offsets, and whether residuals are reconstructed, by the mapping that
the renderer uses). Whether the enhancement picture can be used is only
known when the frame is drawn, and is taken to be as for the frame
before. A frame that turns out to be of another load than it was asked
for with, or that could not be drawn, counts for the comparison of the
load it was asked for with: a load on trial whose frames stopped coming
that way had every frame of the rest of the video drawn the way tried
(172744 of 172800 simulated; now 718). Such a frame goes into a window
of the load it is of only when it was drawn the way of that window; the
six frames after every change of way and after every frame that is not
measured are left out.

Simulated with the times of the integrated GPU (36 ms directly and 30
composed for a picture that fills the frame, 31 and 27.5 letterboxed):
two shapes taking turns every 30 to 1000 frames, and shots of 2 to 6 s
of either, have each shape composed after 186 of its own frames at
most; three to five shapes in turn, 60 to 150 frames each, within ten
seconds of the frames of each. A shape that is on for two seconds in
every 25 is compared with windows made of several visits. With a
letterboxed shape that gains 3 % by composing, and so keeps direct
drawing, the shape that fills the frame is composed all the same, drawn
directly in 180 of its frames of two hours; a light shape (20 ms) is
never tried, and follows the confirmed way. In 60 films of two to five
shapes with times of their own, cut in shots of 20 to 400 frames, with
subtitles and missing times, every shape that takes most of the period
ended in its better way, drawn the worse one for 209 frames at most
when the shapes agree on the way (214 with 3 % of noise on every time
and all times rising and falling by 6 %), and for 348 when each has its
own.

When more than eight loads come, a new one takes the place of one that
is not in a comparison and has no way of its own; of those, the one
that has gathered least (a picture that changes shape gradually is a
new load every few frames, and those take each other's place). A load
that has had no frame measured for ten minutes of film does not keep
its place. A load for which there is no place is drawn the shared way
(sixteen shapes of 60 frames each in turn: eight are compared, and all
are composed from the first confirmation on; 3824 of the 172800 frames
of two hours were drawn directly).

Frames with GUI elements are not measured, and what is being measured
waits for them: a window is made of the frames between two subtitles,
of several gaps when they are short (simulated with a subtitle that is
on and off for 2.5 s each: 178 of the 7200 frames without a subtitle of
ten minutes drawn the slow way). A comparison goes on after them with
what it had; what a load gathered is dropped when it has had no frame
measured for ten minutes.

Neither are the frames of a picture that stands still (the video is
paused or waits for data, and no GUI element is on the screen): it is
drawn again at every refresh with nothing being decoded, which is not
what a frame costs while the video plays. On the integrated GPU such a
frame took 24.7 ms directly and 29.5 composed, where a playing one
takes 35 to 37 and 30: the faster way is the other one, and no refresh
that such a frame misses can be seen. CDVStillPicture tells them: a
picture drawn again more than 200 ms after it was first drawn, by its
time stamp (film at 59.94 Hz has each picture drawn two and three
times, at 120 Hz five, and a late one more). Simulated with a video
that stands still for twenty seconds a minute after composing was
taken, when that is looked at again: measured, those frames took direct
drawing, and 2688 frames (112 s) were drawn the slow way once the video
went on; now 56, the trial that confirms composing. The statistics
count these frames, and leave them out of the times of each way.

A comparison is not kept open at any cost. One that has not ended after
720 frames of its load is given up, and the next one of that load waits
twice as long, also when the way in use does not fit in the period.
With a picture or a GUI that changes faster than a window fills, the
time drawn the way tried stays small (in two simulated hours: five
trials and 3818 frames with cues four frames apart and, every ten
minutes, 60 frames without one; ten trials each and 8400 of the 172800
frames with two loads of which no frame is foreseen as what it is). The
log says when that happens. The wait is that of the load alone (with a
wait for all of them, another shape that came after such a comparison
was given up was drawn the slow way for a minute and a half more), and
does not reach into the next video.

A frame whose GPU time is not known (its timer was still waiting for
the result of an earlier frame, or the result cannot be used) is told
to the choice as well, and counts as a frame of the comparison of its
load: one does not stay open because the times stopped coming. The
results of the timers are taken in the order of the frames.

A trial of the direct way in a video whose picture cannot be drawn
directly (it does not fill the output: the frames asked for directly
come out composed) ends without a result and is not repeated in that
video.

Limits. A load that goes the shared way is not compared while it takes
no more than the heaviest load that confirmed that way, and under four
fifths of the period: if the other way were the faster one for it
alone, it is not found (a shape of 31 ms directly and 26 composed after
one of 30 and 36 confirmed direct drawing stays at 31, which is 74 % of
the period). The choice is there to keep frames within their refresh,
not to find the smallest GPU time. After a comparison was given up, its
load stays the way it was for two to sixteen minutes, also when that is
the slow way. With more than eight picture shapes that each take most
of the period, the ninth has no comparison of its own until one of the
eight has been away for ten minutes. When the ordinary output cannot be
put back at the end of a video (the display refuses it), what was being
measured passes into the next video, and is dropped there ten minutes
later at most. At 60 Hz on the RTX 4080 SUPER direct drawing takes 48
to 54 % of the period: 15 % more for three seconds brings two trials of
composing, under two seconds in all.

The statistics give the GPU time of the frames without GUI elements by
the way they were drawn, and the log says what a comparison found, for
which part of the picture, and whether it is confirmed.
